{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0223ff30572bc3bc0169bb0a38c6e4819195ee30538c54d870", "created": 1773865528, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "(\"entity\"<|>CLINICAL TRIAL<|>event<|>A research study in which people with cancer may enroll to receive and compare specific treatments (standard therapy, new drugs, and sometimes placebo alongside standard treatment). Each trial has eligibility criteria, is run by a research team, requires informed consent, and may involve extra appointments and research-related procedures and costs covered by a study sponsor.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PATIENT<|>character<|>The person with cancer who is considering treatment options, may seek and enroll in a clinical trial, must review and sign informed consent, can ask questions, may discuss decisions with trusted people, and can leave a trial at any time to seek care outside the trial.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PARTICIPANT<|>character<|>A person enrolled in a clinical trial whose health status and treatment outcomes are monitored so the study can compare how disease responds to a specific treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>RESEARCH TEAM<|>character<|>A group of experts who manage a clinical trial, review the study details with the patient/participant, and explain the purpose plus risks and benefits as part of the informed consent process.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>TREATMENT TEAM<|>character<|>The patient’s clinical care providers who discuss treatment options, help determine whether a clinical trial is available and whether the patient meets requirements, and continue standard cancer care during a trial when applicable.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PATHOLOGIST<|>character<|>A medical specialist who diagnoses disease by examining cells/tissue; in this text, the pathologist’s ability to reliably diagnose lymphoma can be affected if steroid therapy changes the appearance of lymphoma cells.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>NCCN EXPERTS<|>character<|>Clinical experts referenced as recommending that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma should consider joining a clinical trial for treatment.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>STUDY SPONSOR<|>character<|>The party that pays for research-related clinical trial costs (including the study drug), while routine care may still be billed to insurance.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>FAMILY AND FRIENDS<|>character<|>Trusted people the patient may consult when deciding whether to join a clinical trial and when reviewing the informed consent form.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE SURGEON<|>character<|>A surgeon who can treat cataracts (a potential side effect associated in the text with intraocular chemotherapy) with a routine operation.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>UNITED STATES<|>place<|>A country referenced as a location for finding clinical trials and cancer centers/resources.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BRAIN<|>place<|>An anatomical location where swelling can occur in lymphoma; steroids can decrease swelling in the brain and reduce signs and symptoms.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>SPINE<|>place<|>An anatomical location assessed (via MRI) for evidence of cancer; findings may influence whether intrathecal chemotherapy is used.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>EYE<|>place<|>An anatomical location where primary CNS lymphoma may rarely occur; chemotherapy may be administered directly into the eye in such cases.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>ARM VEIN<|>place<|>A common access site for intravenous chemotherapy infusion through a tube into a vein in the arm.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BLOODSTREAM<|>place<|>The bodily channel through which intravenously infused medicine travels to reach cells throughout the body.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>CEREBROSPINAL FLUID (CSF)<|>place<|>A body fluid that may contain cancer cells in some cases; chemotherapy may be infused directly into CSF (intrathecal/intra-CSF chemotherapy).)##\n(\"entity\"<|>INFORMED CONSENT FORM<|>object<|>A document providing key clinical trial information (purpose, risks, benefits, and the patient’s role